Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for UN Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, President, and thank you to our briefers.
Colleagues, the October 19 attack in Abyei was the second drone strike on UNISFA’s area of operations in ten months.
This horrific attack, where 11 drone strikes killed innocent civilians and destroyed critical economic infrastructure, is yet another example of how Sudan and South Sudan are failing the people of Abyei. They deserve better from their leaders, and they deserve more from this Council.
The United States sends its deepest condolences to the families who lost loved ones, and we wish the injured a full recovery.
We reiterate that attacks on civilians and civilian objects are prohibited under international humanitarian law. We call for a full, transparent, and prompt investigation, and that the perpetrators of such attacks be held accountable.
We also commend UNISFA’s peacekeepers for their rapid response just hours after the attack.
Two main failures resulted in this attack.
First, unauthorized actors remain in the Abyei Area. Their presence puts civilians in a demilitarized area at grave risk.
Second, Sudan and South Sudan still have not settled Abyei’s final status or implemented the political and security arrangements they committed to in the 2011 Agreement.
The parties must move quickly to ensure Abyei remains stable by withdrawing all unauthorized forces, issuing visas for all UN police, convening regular Joint Political and Security Mechanism and the Abyei Joint Oversight Committee meetings, returning to negotiations on Abyei’s final status, establishing the Abyei Police Service, and fully supporting and cooperating with the Civilian Deputy Head of Mission once appointed.
Every one of these steps is within their power today. The question is not whether they can. It is whether they will.
The UN must also do its part. We urge the Secretariat to move quickly to generate and deploy the first Formed Police Unit, and swiftly recruit and appoint a Civilian Deputy Head of Mission.
For 15 years, this Council accepted slow, incremental steps by the parties. What have they produced? No final status. No demilitarization. No Abyei Police Service. No deployment of the full authorized UN police ceiling.
UNISFA’s mandate gives the Security Council a chance to change this through improved benchmark language that will hold the parties and the UN accountable for real progress related to demilitarization, final status, UN police deployment, and the security and administrative arrangements.
We urge this Council to support these benchmarks as the people of Abyei need results, not another mandate renewal that changes nothing.
