Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for UN Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. The United States unequivocally opposes any ICC actions against U.S. nationals or the nationals of any other U.S. ally that has not consented to ICC jurisdiction.
During his General Debate address in September, President Trump made clear that the United States will not allow its service members to face trials at the ICC and called on all ICC Member States to immediately withdraw from the court.
The core U.S. objections to the ICC have remained unchanged for nearly three decades, going back to our participation in the Rome Statute negotiations.
The ICC prosecutor and judges wield enormous power that has proven to be unchecked, as the most significant safeguards provided in the Rome Statute have failed or have been ignored.
Over the years, the ICC has taken politicized actions against personnel of the United States and its allies that have not consented to its jurisdiction.
The Court’s ongoing actions set a dangerous precedent, threaten to infringe on the principle of state sovereignty, and undermine our critical national security and foreign policy work.
There are other models that can be and have been used to achieve the accountability the ICC’s supporters are seeking.
For example, there are ad hoc or hybrid tribunals established by the UN Security Council or with the consent of relevant states to address specific situations.
These models, while not perfect, carry far less risk than those associated with a permanent, standing institution with a broad mandate and insufficient safeguards. The latter type of institution will be subject to political rather than judicial motivations, and will seek to unjustly expand its power and reach, as we have seen with the ICC.
Other tribunals under the purview of the UN Security Council are better scoped and managed; those that are integrated with national legal systems are better calibrated with national sovereignty. Finally, they are more limited in jurisdiction and duration.
The United States has been clear for over a year as to the basic gestures of good faith we have expected from the Assembly of States Parties; however, neither the ICC nor States Parties have taken meaningful steps to address our concerns.
The draft General Assembly resolution on the ICC annual report we are considering today includes unacceptable elements to expand the reach of the court.
That is why we are voting against the draft resolution and we would encourage others to do the same.
Thank you, Mr. President.
